lesson chemistry-evaporating-to-get-the-salt-back v=2 format=worked grade=3 unit=4 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Evaporating to get the salt back"
  goal "Work out how much water has gone, say where the water and the salt each go, and use what stays the same to answer a question about both."

  example {
    worked chem.evaporate a=300 b=100 easy-level=medium hard-level=medium
    say """
      Take what is left in the beaker away from what was in it to begin with. Nothing was added, so
      the difference is what has gone off as steam. A dish on a sunny windowsill does the same
      thing slowly: the water goes into the air as vapour, without boiling, and the salt stays.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    practice chem.salt-stays count=2 seed=3
    practice chem.evaporate count=2 seed=3
    practice chem.salt-left count=2 seed=5
    practice chem.where-water-go count=2 seed=1
    practice chem.evaporate-faster count=1 seed=4 hard-seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.salt-evidence q=1 day=4 easy-q=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.salt-concentration w=200 g=20 d=3 q=0 easy-w=100 easy-g=10 easy-d=2 easy-q=1 hard-g=30
  }

  grown-ups """
    Salty water left in a saucer on a windowsill for a few days does this without a flame, and the
    crust it leaves is worth looking at through a magnifying glass; it is not tasted. If the water is
    boiled instead, a grown-up does it and the child watches in goggles. The first two questions
    read the dish, which is the way in. The core works out how much water has gone, how much salt is
    left, where each goes, and which of two glasses dries first. The two-star question asks which piece of
    evidence answers a wrong idea, and the two ideas need different evidence. The three-star question
    has two amounts in one story, and only the water changes; the salt in the dish is the salt that
    went in, whether it is still dissolved or has come out as crystals.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the dishes are as written, 150 ml or less has boiled away, the salt left and where each part goes are as written, glass A is on a sunny windowsill against a cool cupboard, the two-star answers Ana with the crust in the dish, and the three-star asks how much water is left after two days."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the dishes are as written, 250 ml or more has boiled away, the salt left and where each part goes are as written, glass A is out in the wind against a still room, and the two-star and three-star are as written, Tom's weighing and the salt that is still all there."
  }
}
